.TH SORTOF-CDCSORT 1 "October 2026" "sortof-suite" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sortof\-cdcsort \- chain identical and near\-identical files behind their first instance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sortof\-cdcsort
[options] SOURCE...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Keeps the list in the order it is given, except that files whose content matches an earlier file are moved right behind it, so a compressor with a small window finds the second copy next to the first. Only the order changes, never the data.
.PP
Identical files (same length and 64\-bit FNV hash) come directly behind the first copy, in the order they appeared. Only files that share a size with another file are read.
.PP
Unless \-s 0, files that are not exact duplicates get a MinHash sketch, and a file whose sketch is at least PCT percent like an earlier file's is moved behind the most similar such file. Candidates come from banding the sketch, with no all\-pairs comparison, so it scales to millions of files. Every file is read once and a sketch needs 512 bytes of memory.
.PP
This is the sorter for cdcstore chunk lists: cdcstore\-list \-k REPO | sortof\-cdcsort \- > list.txt.
.PP
A source is a directory (scanned recursively), a regular file (listed as it is), or \- for a list of paths on standard input, used once. A path that comes up more than once is listed once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s PCT
Chain near matches that are at least PCT percent alike (default 50). \-s 0 chains exact duplicates only.
.TP
.B \-m N
Near\-match candidates tried per band (default 16).
.TP
.B \-e N
Files smaller than N bytes get no sketch (default 512).
.TP
.B \-t N
Threads for reading (default: the number of CPUs).
.TP
.B \-p PATH
Pin PATH: it comes first in the output, exactly as given, and is never sorted, hashed or read. May be repeated. A file named cdcstore.idx is pinned by itself.
.TP
.B \-f PATH
Write the list to PATH instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet.
.TP
.B \-h
Print help.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.RS
cdcstore\-list \-k REPO | sortof\-cdcsort \- > list.txt
.RE
.fi
.sp
